vsh - the shell

Runs what is named: builtins first, then each directory on PATH in turn,
then the tools binary's commands. PATH is /home/bin then /bin, so a
program installed under home is run by typing its name and is found
before one of the same name that shipped. `tools` lists the tool
commands; `help` lists the builtins.

  cd <path>              change directory; `cd` alone goes home
  log | grep fail        pipe one command into another
  date > /tmp/when       write output to a file; >> appends
  kill 12                end a thread by the id `top` shows
  echo "two  words"      one argument, spaces and all

Whitespace separates arguments. Double quotes make what is inside them
one argument, spaces at either end included, which is how a network
password that has one is set. Inside quotes a backslash makes the next
character ordinary, and is the only way to write a quote or a backslash
itself; nowhere else is a backslash special. A quote that is never
closed is refused rather than guessed at.

On the US-International layout the quote is a dead key: press it and
then space for a literal one, which is the same everywhere else on that
layout. On Belgian AZERTY it is on the 3.

Tab completes commands and paths. Up and down walk the history. The
prompt arrow carries the last status: green succeeded, red did not.

A builtin cannot stand in a pipeline: it is the shell itself and has
no process of its own to give the pipe to.
